# Worked Examples — Junior to Mid/Senior

Companion to `production-code-best-practices.md`. Every principle there, shown as code here: the junior version, the production version, and *why* the difference matters. Read the bad version first and try to spot the problem before reading the fix — that's the reviewer muscle you're training.

---

## 1. JavaScript

### 1.1 The floating promise (the most common real-world async bug)

```js
// ❌ Junior: looks fine, works in dev, crashes prod
app.post('/signup', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await createUser(req.body);
  sendWelcomeEmail(user.email); // async, but not awaited or caught
  res.status(201).json(user);
});
```

Problems: (1) if `sendWelcomeEmail` rejects, that's an unhandled rejection — Node kills the process; (2) errors vanish silently until then; (3) reviewers can't tell if the missing `await` is intentional.

```js
// ✅ Production: fire-and-forget is fine — but make it EXPLICIT and SAFE
app.post('/signup', async (req, res) => {
  const user = await createUser(req.body);
  // intentionally not awaited: email must not block or fail the signup
  void sendWelcomeEmail(user.email).catch((err) =>
    logger.error({ err, userId: user.id }, 'welcome email failed'),
  );
  res.status(201).json(user);
});
```

Senior move: don't send email in the request at all — enqueue a job (see §4.7). The request path should do the minimum work needed to respond correctly.

### 1.2 Sequential awaits — the accidental 10x slowdown

```js
// ❌ 3 round trips, one after another (~300ms if each is 100ms)
const user = await getUser(id);
const orders = await getOrders(id);
const prefs = await getPrefs(id);
```

```js
// ✅ Independent work runs in parallel (~100ms)
const [user, orders, prefs] = await Promise.all([
  getUser(id),
  getOrders(id),
  getPrefs(id),
]);
```

But know when sequential is *correct*: when call N+1 needs call N's result, or when you're rate-limited. And know the next trap:

```js
// ❌ Unbounded parallelism: 50,000 rows = 50,000 simultaneous DB queries
await Promise.all(rows.map((row) => processRow(row)));

// ✅ Bounded concurrency
import pLimit from 'p-limit';
const limit = pLimit(10);
await Promise.all(rows.map((row) => limit(() => processRow(row))));
```

`Promise.all` also rejects on the *first* failure while the rest keep running. When partial failure is acceptable (batch import, notifications), use `allSettled` and report:

```js
const results = await Promise.allSettled(jobs);
const failed = results.filter((r) => r.status === 'rejected');
if (failed.length) logger.warn({ count: failed.length },

